01:02:29.599 --> 01:02:31.910
uh that idea goes something like this.

01:02:31.920 --> 01:02:33.910
PO has some clipping terms on it that

01:02:33.920 --> 01:02:36.950
are meant as an off policy correction.

01:02:36.960 --> 01:02:38.230
They're intended to prevent you from

01:02:38.240 --> 01:02:40.309
collapsing your policy by iterating on

01:02:40.319 --> 01:02:43.270
very slightly stale data. However, if

01:02:43.280 --> 01:02:45.910
you crank up the amount of sample reuse,

01:02:45.920 --> 01:02:47.750
you count up crank up the number of

01:02:47.760 --> 01:02:50.789
update epochs, that is, you will just

01:02:50.799 --> 01:02:53.510
collapse the entire algorithm.

01:02:53.520 --> 01:02:55.750
This seems like an algorithmic defect to

01:02:55.760 --> 01:02:57.990
me. If we're going to have clipping

01:02:58.000 --> 01:03:01.829
terms to prevent us from using data that

01:03:01.839 --> 01:03:04.390
is too stale,

01:03:04.400 --> 01:03:06.470
then we should be able to crank up the

01:03:06.480 --> 01:03:09.750
number of batches as much as we want.

01:03:09.760 --> 01:03:12.230
And when updates are too stale, the

01:03:12.240 --> 01:03:13.990
algorithm should just be able to zero

01:03:14.000 --> 01:03:15.510
those.

01:03:15.520 --> 01:03:18.549
That was the idea. And this is important

01:03:18.559 --> 01:03:20.309
because first of all, it takes a

01:03:20.319 --> 01:03:22.710
hyperparameter that's the replay use a

01:03:22.720 --> 01:03:25.190
replay ratio and it makes it stable. So

01:03:25.200 --> 01:03:26.789
if you increase it, you'll never break

01:03:26.799 --> 01:03:29.670
anything. Um it's an important

01:03:29.680 --> 01:03:31.670
invariance where I think that not having

01:03:31.680 --> 01:03:34.150
